
Representable object bounds (option iii, 2026-10-17). Three further changes, each necessary and
none sufficient alone: seven size-class strides raised by 1-4 units so every large class's stride
is a multiple of its own bounds granule; a granule-sized group header so the first slot's base is a
multiple of it too; and enframe stepping each object's base DOWN to a granule-aligned offset within
its slot. Without them the compressed encoder truncates an unaligned base DOWNWARD and the object
gains authority below itself -- measured against the RTL bounds model over 6060 (class, request,
slot, offset-cycle) cases, 1497 decode wrong with the step-down alone and a 16-byte header, the
first being class 36 at n=16480 slot 0 offset 0, whose base 0x400000010 decodes as
[0x400000000, 0x400004080), and 0 with all three in place.

The header costs nothing ON THE MULTI-SLOT PATH: alloc_group rounds the group UP to a page, and
over every class and every reachable cnt that rounding leaves at least the pad. The margin is exact
in places (class 36 at cnt 7 has 16 bytes for a 16-byte pad), so cap_hdr_fits asserts it and a
future table or count change fails loudly instead of overrunning the group. Measured: 0 of 10,908
multi-slot sizes overrun. The step-down is down and never up, so it cannot exceed slack, and it is
a no-op for any granule at or below UNIT -- every length below 8 KiB -- so the double-free offset
cycling is untouched there.

CORRECTION (2026-10-17): an earlier version of this paragraph said the header costs nothing, full
stop, and that was wrong for the two SINGLE-SLOT shapes, where it cost every allocation. They have
no page-rounding slack at all: alloc_group's cnt==1 branch maps exactly pageround(n+IB+UNIT) and
the >= MMAP_THRESHOLD path maps n+IB+UNIT, and each then binds one slot covering the whole mapping
after the header -- so the slot ended granule-UNIT bytes PAST the mapping, bind_slots could not
derive the shrink, and malloc returned NULL. Over 300,000 request sizes: 100% of the large path,
from n=131052 up, and 95.5% of the single-slot group path, from n=16365 up. cap_hdr_fits was blind
to all 114,687 of the group cases because it is evaluated on the CLASS stride and on
needed = size*cnt+UNIT, neither of which those paths map -- an assert that could not fire for the
shape that needed it.

cap_single_slot_bytes now reserves the header for both, as a fixed point: the header depends on the
stride, the stride on the mapping size. It is also made a fixed point of the BOUNDS rounding,
because __capstone_malloc_map applies capstone_repr_round and a merely page-aligned size can be
granted a larger mapping than maplen*4096 records (6303744 becomes 6307840 at a granule of 8192),
which would leave get_stride and bind_slots disagreeing about where the slot ends. get_stride
subtracts the granule header rather than UNIT for these groups, so enframe's slack, free's bounds
check, realloc, malloc_usable_size and aligned_alloc all see the stride that was actually bound.
Verified over 300,003 sizes, in the model and again in C: converges in at most 3 rounds, 0
overruns, 0 capacity failures, 0 sizes where the granted mapping differs from maplen*4096.

size_to_class and the slot/class selection are untouched: the request is rounded in the runtime's
own malloc wrapper, outside musl, so set_size records exactly what the capability covers.
check-mallocng-policy.py pins the seven entries and their values and refuses any other difference.

These live here rather than in a sibling patch because build-sdk.sh checks currency by requiring
every patch to reverse-apply INDEPENDENTLY, and a patch editing the lines this one adds makes this
one un-reversible -- the SDK build then refuses with "musl source is not current".
